BEHAVIOUR 16 IS GENUINELY FIXED — RE-TESTED BY HAND, NOT READ

Two git repos in $TMPDIR with deliberately different trees: `outer`, holding a
copy of examples/sample-studio as its officina, and `inner`, an unrelated repo.
`red` invoked from /home/edckt/projects/bisellium — a third repo — with
`--studio <outer>/studio --cwd <inner>`:

  recorded header                    # tree: tree:20b73b17568d4fe6cc40ca5648c273c8c6b67697
  inner's tree  (sourceTreeHash)     20b73b17568d4fe6cc40ca5648c273c8c6b67697   <- match
  outer's tree, no excludes          aa80cc7c4ec1829ee6dc51a927da99489d9a209f
  outer's tree, studio excluded      9e886ab602f8b3a507a3c841cd1fa2b25e990695
  wrapped command's own process.cwd  <inner>

The header names the repo the command actually ran in, under either exclusion
set, and not the officina's parent. That is exactly the defect W-021's round-3
review raised (a red certifying the caller's tree instead of the exercised
tree) and it is closed. Three further edges, all correct:

  --cwd at a directory in no git repo   -> `# tree: none`, not a silent
                                           fallback to something unasked for
  --repo <outer> with --cwd <inner>     -> tree:9e886ab6…, the explicit
                                           override winning, officina excluded
  no --cwd, no --repo                   -> the caller's own repo, dirty-marked

And `red` still refuses a command that exits 0 (exit 1, nothing written).
That refusal remains the best thing in this opus.

REDS 09, 10 AND 11 ARE ASSERTION-LEVEL — CONFIRMED

Three distinct documents (hashes e574641c, 8acc2abc, c45f45003, computed with
the `# behaviour:` line stripped), each naming its own behaviour's assertions,
each having RUN rather than failed to load. `# tree:` on all three is
dirty:6788b647…, the stubbed worktree, not the officina's parent. Round 4's
acceptance of these holds and I confirm it independently.

One thing round 4 flagged as a caution is still visible in the artifacts, and
I record it as a finding rather than let it pass silently:

  10.log  PASS  red: no directory created for a passing command
  11.log  PASS  red: none of the usage refusals created ci/reds

A stub that does nothing creates nothing, so these two silence-only assertions
are trivially true against it. They are green inside a document filed as a red.
Behaviours 10 and 11 are therefore only partially red at assertion level. Not
blocking on its own — the other assertions in both files did run and did fail —
but it is the trap W-021's review named and builder-b avoided by stubbing to a
failing value rather than to nothing. Whoever re-runs 01-08 and 12 should stub
that way, and should re-run 10 and 11 in the same pass.


